button><button type="button" data-testid="qr-code-button" aria-label="QR Code" class="TopicHelperIcons_qrCode__hEEgG btn btn-light"><span style="box-sizing:border-box;display:inline-block;overflow:hidden;width:initial;height:initial;background:none;opacity:1;border:0;margin:0;padding:0;position:relative;max-width:100%"><span style="box-sizing:border-box;display:block;width:initial;height:initial;background:none;opacity:1;border:0;margin:0;padding:0;max-width:100%"><img style="display:block;max-width:100%;width:initial;height:initial;background:none;opacity:1;border:0;margin:0;padding:0" alt="" aria-hidden="true" src="data:image/svg+xml,%3csvg%20xmlns=%27http://www.w3.org/2000/svg%27%20version=%271.1%27%20width=%2729%27%20height=%2729%27/%3e"/></span><img alt="QR Code" data-pin-nopin="true" role="img" srcSet="/home/_next/static/media/qr-code-svgrepo-com.7c911e42.svg?w=32 1x, /home/_next/static/media/qr-code-svgrepo-com.7c911e42.svg?w=64 2x" src="/home/_next/static/media/qr-code-svgrepo-com.7c911e42.svg?w=64" decoding="async" data-nimg="intrinsic" style="position:absolute;top:0;left:0;bottom:0;right:0;box-sizing:border-box;padding:0;border:none;margin:auto;display:block;width:0;height:0;min-width:100%;max-width:100%;min-height:100%;max-height:100%"/></span></button></div></span><div class="TopicHead_topic__authors_main_cont__C4Qif"><span class="TopicHead_topic__revision__InHgd false false">Full Review:<span class="downloadContent skipdownloadmd"> May 2026</span></span><span class="TopicHead_topic__authors__description__hdr9y"><span> By</span><span class="TopicHead_authorName__64Yix"><a href="/home/authors/jordan-jaime" class="TopicHead_authorsName__hGr8H undefined">Jaime Jordan</a>, <!-- -->MD<!-- -->, <span class="TopicHead_authorAffiliation__rsPe4">UCLA School of Medicine</span></span><span class="TopicHead_topic__authors__description__hdr9y false"></span></span><div class="TopicHead_topic__revision__InHgd false false"><span class="downloadContent skipdownloadmd">Last updated: May 2026</span></div><span class="TopicHead_onlydownload__Uw_hT downloadContent skipdownloadmd">v829525</span></div></div><div class="TopicHead_rightBlockContainer__wkSFt"><!--$--><div tabindex="0" class="TopicHead_topic_version___ht5T TopicHead_topic__switch_version__top__0FSPa" aria-label="VIEW PROFESSIONAL VERSION" role="link"><div class="TopicHead_switch_version__container__7IWnG"><i class="TopicHead_switch_version__icon__aK97g"></i><div class="TopicHead_switchVersionText__odSPM">VIEW PROFESSIONAL VERSION</div></div></div> <!--/$--></div></div></div><div data-testid="topic-main-content" class="TopicMainContent_content__rtl23   "><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829538" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Cuts or tears in tissue (lacerations), scrapes (abrasions), and puncture wounds can be caused by contact with sharp objects, </span><span data-testid="topicXlink"><span><a class="TopicXLink_title__Oh7al consumer undefined" href="/home/injuries-and-poisoning/bites-and-stings/introduction-to-bites-and-stings" data-testid="topic-xlink-anchor"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">bites</span></a></span></span><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">, or by other mechanisms. Wounds that are not caused by bites and are clean and relatively small usually heal rapidly without any problems. However, some wounds can cause extensive blood loss. </span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v38403703" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">In some wounds, deeper structures, such as nerves, tendons, or blood vessels, are also injured. Wounds can become infected. A piece of foreign material (such as a splinter, glass, or a clothing fragment) can also remain hidden inside a wound, causing later problems such as infection, prolonged pain, or delayed healing.</span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829540" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Shallow cuts to most areas of the skin rarely bleed much and often stop bleeding on their own. Cuts to the hand and scalp as well as cuts to arteries and larger veins often bleed vigorously.</span></p><div data-testid="pearlDidyouknow" dir="ltr" class="TopicPearlDidYouKnow_pearlDidyouknow__J0GKw undefined consumer false"><div dir="ltr" class="TopicPearlDidYouKnow_pearlDidyouknowContainer__yJcyK undefined printBreakInside"><div class="TopicPearlDidYouKnow_pearlDidyouknowInner__8XIdn TopicPearlDidYouKnow_DidYouKnow__doZSs"><div data-testid="BaseTableView" class="undefined TopicTableView_didYouKnow__QQEqr"><div class="TopicTableView_ariaAnnouncement__prBWM" aria-live="polite"></div><div tabindex="-1"><h3 class="undefined TableHeader_title__REY9f false false
      TableHeader_tableHeaderWithoutScroll__Xnmx_
      undefined skipdownloadmd" id="v8381018"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Did You Know...</span></p></h3></div><div tabindex="-1" class="TopicTableView_tableWrap__ZzPqw    "><table class="TopicTableView_tableViewWrapper__NC6RG "><tbody class="undefined"><tr data-testid="TopicTableBodyView" class=""><td data-testid="TopicTableCellView" rowspan="" colSpan="" class="TopicTableCellView_tableData__pQ2zv TopicTableCellView_notinpopup__lcznU "><ul class="TopicList_topicList__nW6kG undefined
            TopicList_tableListView__w4iky undefined" data-testid="topiclistList"><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v55358855" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Even shallow cuts to the scalp or fingertip may bleed vigorously.</span></p></li></ul></td></tr></tbody></table><div class="TopicTableView_mediaprintfooter__cNGyB false"></div></div></div></div></div></div><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829541" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Infection can develop when a wound is contaminated with dirt and bacteria. Although any wound can become infected, infection is particularly likely in deep scrapes, which grind dirt into the skin, and in puncture wounds (particularly those resulting from </span><span data-testid="topicXlink"><span><a class="TopicXLink_title__Oh7al consumer undefined" href="/home/injuries-and-poisoning/bites-and-stings/animal-bites" data-testid="topic-xlink-anchor"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">animal bites</span></a></span></span><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C"> or </span><span data-testid="topicXlink"><span><a class="TopicXLink_title__Oh7al consumer undefined" href="/home/injuries-and-poisoning/bites-and-stings/human-bites" data-testid="topic-xlink-anchor"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">human bites</span></a></span></span><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">), which introduce contamination deep under the skin. Also, wounds that contain foreign material frequently become infected. The longer a wound remains contaminated, the more likely it is that infection will develop.</span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829542" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Wounds can be painful at first, but usually the pain lessens after the first day. If a cut affects a nerve or tendon, the person may be unable to move the body part fully. Some nerve injuries cause weakness or paralysis, loss of sensation, or numbness. If foreign material remains inside a puncture wound, the part of the wound near the material is usually painful when touched.</span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829543" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Pain that worsens a day or more after the injury is often the first sign of infection. Later, an infected wound may become red and swollen and may ooze pus. A fever may also develop.</span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v38403707" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicXlink"><span><a class="TopicXLink_title__Oh7al consumer undefined" href="/home/infections/bacterial-skin-infections/necrotizing-skin-infections" data-testid="topic-xlink-anchor"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Necrotizing skin infections</span></a></span></span><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C"> are rapidly progressing infections that threaten life and limb that can occur even after a very minor skin wound.</span></p><span id="first-aid-for-cuts,-scrapes,-and-other-wounds" class="TopicFHead_topicFHeadSectionWithoutScroll__v86E8" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-testid="topicFHeadAnchor"></span><span id="v829544" class="TopicFHead_topicFHeadSectionWithoutScroll__v86E8" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-testid="topicFHeadAnchor"></span><span id="v829545" class="TopicFHead_topicFHeadSectionWithoutScroll__v86E8" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true" data-testid="topicFHeadAnchor"></span><section id="First-Aid-for-Cuts,-Scrapes,-and-Other-Wounds_v829544" itemProp="" itemscope="" itemType="" class=" consumer TopicFHead_topicFHeadSectionIdWithoutScroll__68joZ" data-testid="topicFHeadSection"><h2 id="Treatment_v829545" class="TopicFHead_topicFHead___lHdE
          consumer sticky-fhead readable downloadContent  undefined" data-originaltitle="First Aid for Cuts, Scrapes, and Other Wounds" data-testid="topicFHeadHeading"><span class="TopicFHead_topicFHeadTitle__6uLUl"><span>First Aid for Cuts, Scrapes, and Other Wounds</span></span><span class="hidden-span"></span></h2><div class="TopicFHead_fHeadBody__wxFX8" data-testid="fheadbody"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829546" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">The first step in treating a cut is to stop the bleeding. Visible bleeding can almost always be stopped by placing a gauze pad or clean cloth over the bleeding area and firmly compressing the area with a finger or hand for at least 5 minutes. Whenever possible, the bleeding part is elevated above the level of the heart. </span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="x000012" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If the bleeding is on an arm or leg and continues after pressure has been applied, a tourniquet can be applied by anyone who has been trained to use one. This is usually required only in severe injuries (for example, gunshot wounds, deep penetrating stab injuries, large deep cuts, or amputations. Tourniquets should be used only until the person can be evaluated and treated by a doctor. (See </span><span><a class="TopicLink_topicLink__qJmYk consumer undefined" href="https://www.stopthebleed.org/" target="" rel="" data-testid="topicLink">Stop the Bleed.org</a></span><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C"> for additional information on tourniquet use.)</span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829548" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">To prevent infection, dirt and particles are removed and the wound is washed. Large, visible particles are picked off. Smaller dirt and particles that cannot be seen are removed by washing with mild soap and tap water. Gentle scrubbing may be required to cleanse deep scrapes. Colder temperature water may allow the blood vessels to constrict, helping to slow the bleeding. Dirt and particles that remain after washing often can be removed with a more highly pressured stream of warm tap water. Harsher agents, such as alcohol, iodine, and peroxide, are not recommended. These solutions can damage tissue, impairing the capacity to heal. </span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v38403709" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If a wound is very small, it can be kept closed with first aid tape or skin adhesive (products that are used specifically for closing a small wound). Stitches may be needed for deep or large cuts. Stitches should be placed only by a health care professional. After cleansing and, if necessary, closing the wound, antibiotic ointment and a bandage are applied. Antibiotic ointments should not be applied if specialized tape or skin adhesive has been used for wound closure.</span></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829549" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Medical assistance is needed under the following circumstances:</span></p><ul class="TopicList_topicList__nW6kG undefined
            undefined undefined" data-testid="topiclistList"><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v1380831" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If a cut is longer than about ⅓ inch (¾ centimeter), is on the face, appears deep, or has edges that separate</span></p></li><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v1380832" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If bleeding does not stop on its own or within several minutes after pressure is applied</span></p></li><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v1380833" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If there are symptoms of a nerve or tendon injury, such as loss of sensation or loss of movement</span></p></li><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v1380834" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If a scrape is deep or has dirt and particles that are difficult to remove</span></p></li><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v1380835" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If there is a puncture wound, particularly if foreign material in the wound is likely</span></p></li><li data-testid="topicListItem"><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v1380836" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If the person has not had a </span><span data-testid="topicXlink"><span><a class="TopicXLink_title__Oh7al consumer undefined" href="/home/infections/bacterial-infections-anaerobic-bacteria/tetanus#v81568178" data-testid="topic-xlink-anchor"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">tetanus vaccination</span></a></span></span><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C"> within the past 5 years</span></p></li></ul><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v829557" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">All wounds, whether treated at home or by health care professionals, should be observed for symptoms of infection (worsening redness, tenderness, warmth, or drainage of pus) during the first several days after treatment. </span><i data-testid="TopicItalic"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">If any symptoms of infection develop (such as fever, pus in or leaking from the wound, or redness increasing around the wound), medical assistance should be sought as quickly as possible (within several hours).</span></i></p><p class=" TopicPara_topicParaWithoutScroll__yzic5 readable 
      downloadContent TopicPara_topicPara__06ZHa TopicPara_inlineSpacingFix__tKAA9 para" id="v38403711" data-testid="topicPara"><span data-testid="topicText" class="TopicPara_topicText__G6F4C">Most small wounds heal within a few days.</span></p></div></section></div><div id="additionalcontent-414CFD515AFF42929A806BD77EC1A73C" class="Topic_autoloadElem__csgXW"></div><div class="Topic_topicAdditionalContent__iJt6y false"><div class=" TwoColumnContainer_twoColumnContainer__dDt1L
  false false 
      false false
